  "panels": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "About",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 4
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Upstream safety controls.** Two independent protections, both exercised by the steady sim workload when `SIM_LIMIT_DEMO=1`.\n\n- **Ancestry-depth circuit breaker** — a self-recursive `RecursiveProbe` chain climbs via `enqueueChild` until an enqueue exceeds the max execution depth (default 10) and is rejected as `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`, incrementing `anvilmq_ancestry_rejections_total`. Lineages that outgrow the runaway-chain cap instead hit `anvilmq_chain_quarantines_total`.\n- **Dispatch-side faceted throttle** — a fixed-window claim-rate rule on facet `tenant:dispatch-throttle`. Over-rate jobs are admitted but not claimed; each poll that skips a due-but-throttled job increments `anvilmq_throttled_polls_total`. Per-facet throttle state is also queryable live via the `GetRateLimitStatus` RPC (`is_throttled`), which has no Prometheus series.\n\n**Rejected enqueues never create a job** (the guards return before the insert), so they leave no row in `/v1/failures` or `/v1/jobs/:id`. The only server-side breadcrumb is the broker's warn log — see the **Enqueue-rejection logs** panel at the bottom (from Loki), where each line's structured fields (`name`, `trace_id`, `parent_id`, `execution_depth`) identify the offending job and lineage."
      }
    },
    {
